\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\title{Semantic representation diagnostics along pretraining checkpoints}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{This bounded diagnostic records when a declared semantic representation
becomes recoverable along a pretraining trajectory. It uses the spatial and
temporal probing design of \citet{Sections 2-4 and 7}{gurnee2024space} as a
measurement template. The result is a representation diagnostic, not a claim
about a developmental mechanism, a deployment capability, or a post-training
law.}

\p{The task family is fixed before inspecting checkpoints: the six coordinate
task datasets in the source family (world, United States, and New York City
locations; historical figures, cultural works, and news headlines). Each score
uses held-out entities. An eligible model family supplies at least three
ordered pretraining checkpoints, identical train, validation, and test entity
splits and filtering at every checkpoint, and the same layer-wise linear ridge-probe protocol, including a predeclared
ridge-regularization grid and validation selection rule. A family or checkpoint
missing one of those fields is
reported as missing rather than silently pooled.}

\p{For model family #{m}, checkpoint #{c}, task #{q}, and layer #{\ell}, let
#{s^{\mathrm{val}}_{m,c,q,\ell}} and #{s^{\mathrm{test}}_{m,c,q,\ell}}
be validation and held-out-test coefficients of determination for the fixed
probe. Choose the layer on validation entities, then evaluate once on the test
entities:}
##{
\ell^*_{m,c,q}=\arg\max_{\ell\in L_m}s^{\mathrm{val}}_{m,c,q,\ell},\qquad
s_{m,c,q}=s^{\mathrm{test}}_{m,c,q,\ell^*_{m,c,q}}.
}
\p{A predeclared layer may replace the validation choice, but a test score is
never used to select #{\ell^*}; this keeps the emergence score from being a
layer-selection envelope.}
\p{The primary absolute emergence threshold is #{\tau=0.50} in this held-out
score, chosen before the trajectory is inspected. The first crossing is}
##{
e_{m,q}(\tau)=\min\{c:s_{m,c,q}\geq\tau\},
}
\p{with #{e_{m,q}(\tau)=\bot} when no checkpoint crosses. A crossing is
reported with the checkpoint token count, layer, a bootstrap uncertainty
interval over held-out entities, and the probe seed; it is not smoothed into a
claim about an unobserved training
interval. Sensitivity must include #{\tau\in\{0.25,0.50,0.75\}}, model family,
parameter scale, and checkpoint spacing.}

\p{The structural record contains held-out coordinate prediction, block and
cross-entity generalization, prompt variants, and the layer at which the score
peaks. These are content and representation probes. A function or deployment
record is separate: it must specify an intervention or user task, an outcome
metric, matched controls, and its own cost and failure accounting. A probe
crossing alone cannot populate that record.}

\p{The source reports linear spatial and temporal representations across
Llama-2 and Pythia scales, prompt sensitivity, cross-entity robustness, and
individual neurons; see \citet{Sections 2.1-2.3, 3.1-3.3, 4.1-4.2, and 5}{gurnee2024space}.
Those observations motivate the fields above, but every trajectory association
reported by this protocol is noncausal. In particular, it makes no claim about
which training environment caused the score, whether a representation is used
by a deployed function, or what post-training will preserve.}

\p{Each trajectory record therefore retains the model family and size,
checkpoint token count, task and entity split, probe class and seed, all layer
scores, threshold grid, missingness, and the separately reported function or
deployment assay. Comparisons are within the declared task family and model
family before any cross-family summary. A change in checkpoint spacing or
probe protocol is a new arm, not evidence of an earlier or later emergence.}

\p{This protocol links the conceptual-discovery question in
\ref{ftip-00MH} to the capability coordinates in \ref{ftip-00JM}; the panel
of open comparison questions in \ref{ftip-00M2} transcludes this child.}
